      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naper%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named after Ralph Naper, one of its original landowners.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naper%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2003, the Naper Historical Society was formed to preserve the town’s heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.napernebraska.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1889 treaty opened the land north of the Niobrara River for settlement, paving the way for Naper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.napernebraska.org/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Naper and George Hotaling each gave up 40 acres of their homesteads to create the townsite.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
